/**
 * The shelf — 62 real products sold in India, with the price, the ingredients
 * and the page they can be bought from.
 *
 * WHAT THIS REPLACED. Thirteen products under one invented house brand,
 * "Together Beauty Labs", written to exercise the recommender. They did that
 * job and they could not do the next one: a citizen who is told to use a
 * Ceramide Barrier Cream cannot go and buy a Ceramide Barrier Cream. Every row
 * here is a product with a brand, a real price in rupees, and a link to where
 * it is sold.
 *
 * IT IS GENERATED FROM THE OWNER'S DATA SHEET, AND EDITED BY HAND AFTERWARDS IS
 * A MISTAKE. The sheet carries brand, name, price, tier, skin/hair type, key
 * ingredients, benefits, a photograph and a product page. Five fields the
 * ENGINE needs are not in it and are derived here — `usage`, `suitableSkin`,
 * `profileKeys`, `tags` and the display `category` — so the next sheet should
 * be re-run through the same derivation rather than diffed against this file.
 *
 * THE FOUR DERIVATIONS, BECAUSE THEY ARE JUDGEMENTS AND NOT FACTS:
 *
 *   `usage` comes from the category, then two overrides: a retinoid is Night
 *   whatever else it is, and vitamin C is Morning, because that is where its
 *   antioxidant protection has something to do. Shampoo, conditioner and hair
 *   oil are Weekly — they are wash-day items, and the routine's weekly band is
 *   named for both.
 *
 *   `suitableSkin` gates matching HARD: a product whose list excludes you is
 *   never recommended, at any score. So only FACE products are gated at all —
 *   a shampoo that suited people with oily FACES would be a bug wearing a data
 *   field — "all skin types" anywhere in the sheet's own wording means `all`,
 *   ranges like "dry to normal" expand, and three of the four base types is
 *   treated as universal, because at that point the list is excluding one type
 *   by accident rather than on purpose.
 *
 *   `profileKeys` are the assessment reading keys this product answers, matched
 *   on ingredients and benefits, then filtered by group: a face product cannot
 *   claim `scalp`, a hair product cannot claim `acne`. Body products are
 *   allowed only hydration, texture and redness. Where nothing matched, the
 *   category supplies one key rather than leaving the product unmatchable —
 *   a product with no keys can never be recommended to anybody, which is a
 *   silent way to delete it.
 *
 *   `tags` are the biomarker refinement and only ever add to an existing
 *   profile match. Hair tags are refused to non-hair products (caffeine in a
 *   face cream was being read as hair density) and `spf` only to a sunscreen.
 *
 * THE PHOTOGRAPHS ARE HOTLINKED to the retailers' own CDNs, which is how the
 * sheet supplies them, and every row carries TWO — a primary and an alternate
 * from a different retailer. That is not belt and braces: a hotlinked image is
 * the one field here guaranteed to rot, and a shelf of broken frames reads as a
 * broken shop rather than a missing picture. The screens try the first, fall to
 * the second, and end at a category mark, which is a real answer rather than a
 * grey box with a torn corner in it.
 */
